var _markdown = "\n\nA page is a single file in `src/ui/pages/` that exports one `definePage(...)` call. Pages are discovered by convention — the file's path *is* the route, so there is no registration step and no route table to keep in sync. Everything a page needs — data, guards, loading and error UI, meta, and the component itself — is declared in one plain, serializable object.\n\nBecause pages are the unit of both rendering and routing, the same file drives **server-side rendering** (loaders run on the server, HTML streams out) and **client-side rendering** in `api+spa` mode. There is no separate server page and client page: one `definePage`, two execution contexts. See [Server-Side Rendering](/docs/rendering/ssr) for how the file runs on the server.\n\n## The definePage Factory [#the-definepage-factory]\n\n`definePage` comes from `@kwiva/react`. Its options cover the complete page lifecycle — from search-param validation before the route is even accepted, to the loader that fetches data, to the `<head>` that ships with the first paint:\n\n```tsx title=\"the-definepage-factory.tsx\"\nimport { definePage, Link, useNavigate, notFound, stream, redirect } from '@kwiva/react'\n\nexport default definePage({\n  // validation\n  validateSearch: (s) => s.object({ q: s.optional(s.string()), page: s.optional(s.number()) }),\n\n  // data\n  loader: async ({ params, search, client }) => ({\n    post: await client.posts.get(params.id),\n    comments: stream(client.comments.list({ postId: params.id })),   // deferred\n  }),\n  loaderDeps: ({ search: { page } }) => ({ page }),                   // refetch only on page change\n\n  // guards\n  beforeLoad: ({ session, location }) => {\n    if (!session.user) throw redirect({ to: '/login', search: { back: location.href } })\n  },\n\n  // UI states\n  pendingComponent: PostSkeleton,\n  pendingMs: 200, pendingMinMs: 300,\n  errorComponent: ({ error }) => <ErrorPanel error={error} />,\n  notFoundComponent: () => <p>No such post</p>,\n\n  // meta\n  head: ({ loaderData }) => ({\n    title: loaderData.post.title,\n    meta: [{ name: 'description', content: loaderData.post.excerpt }],\n  }),\n\n  // the component\n  component: PostPage,\n})\n```\n\nThe full option surface, field by field:\n\n| Option                       | Purpose                                                                        |\n| ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `validateSearch`             | Schema for search state; typed into `Route.useSearch()`, links, and navigation |\n| `loader`                     | Async data loader; runs server-side during SSR, client-side during navigation  |\n| `loaderDeps`                 | Scopes loader re-execution to a slice of the search state                      |\n| `beforeLoad`                 | Guard running before loaders — redirects and permission checks                 |\n| `pendingComponent`           | Rendered while loaders run during navigation                                   |\n| `pendingMs` / `pendingMinMs` | Debounce floor and minimum display time for the pending state                  |\n| `errorComponent`             | Route-scoped error boundary, receiving the thrown `error`                      |\n| `notFoundComponent`          | Rendered for the typed `notFound()` outcome                                    |\n| `head`                       | Document metadata, derived from loader data per route                          |\n| `component`                  | The page component, receiving typed `loaderData`                               |\n\n## From File to Route [#from-file-to-route]\n\nThe route tree is generated from the file system. Dotted filenames collapse into path segments, `$` prefixes a dynamic segment, and folders nest layouts:\n\n| File                              | Route             |\n| --------------------------------- | ----------------- |\n| `src/ui/pages/index.tsx`          | `/`               |\n| `src/ui/pages/about.tsx`          | `/about`          |\n| `src/ui/pages/posts.index.tsx`    | `/posts`          |\n| `src/ui/pages/posts.$id.tsx`      | `/posts/:id`      |\n| `src/ui/pages/posts.$id.edit.tsx` | `/posts/:id/edit` |\n| `src/ui/pages/files.$.tsx`        | `/files/*`        |\n\nThe route file name becomes the route identity: `posts.$id.tsx` declares a `$id` param that the loader reads as `params.id`, and the search schema it validates becomes the search state of every link pointing at it. See [File-Based Routing](/docs/frontend/routing) for the full naming reference.\n\n## The Page Component [#the-page-component]\n\n`component` receives everything the page declared. Loader results arrive as typed `loaderData`, params come from `Route.useParams()`, and search state is typed by `validateSearch`:\n\n```tsx title=\"the-page-component.tsx\"\nconst { id } = Route.useParams()\nconst search = Route.useSearch()\nconst loaderData = Route.useLoaderData()\n```\n\nRoute hooks mirror the loaders' types exactly, because both are generated from the same file. The component is plain — build it however you like, as long as it reads from the typed inputs. Complex interactive surfaces compose from the [Data Hooks](/docs/frontend/data-hooks) on top of the loader's results rather than duplicating them.\n\n## Head & Meta [#head--meta]\n\nSet document-level metadata with the `head` option. Because it is a function of `loaderData`, the tags can describe the actual resource being rendered — an SEO description built from the post's excerpt, a page title from its heading:\n\n```tsx title=\"head-meta.tsx\"\nhead: ({ loaderData }) => ({\n  title: loaderData.post.title,\n  meta: [{ name: 'description', content: loaderData.post.excerpt }],\n})\n```\n\nThe `head` is serialized **per route on the server**, so the first HTML response already carries the correct document metadata. Because it is a function of loader data, cached pages keep correct SEO tags without re-rendering — see [Caching Strategies](/docs/rendering/caching).\n\n## Loading, Error, and Not Found UI [#loading-error-and-not-found-ui]\n\nEvery page gets opinionated fallback UI without extra wiring:\n\n| Option                       | Purpose                                                                                |\n| ---------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `pendingComponent`           | Rendered while loaders run during navigation                                           |\n| `pendingMs` / `pendingMinMs` | Delay a fast route from flashing the pending state, and floor its minimum display time |\n| `errorComponent`             | Route-scoped error boundary, receiving the thrown `error`                              |\n| `notFoundComponent`          | Rendered when the route (or a loader) throws the typed `notFound()`                    |\n\n```plaintext title=\"loading-error-and-not-found-ui.txt\"\nPending shown only if data takes longer than pendingMs\n   └─ commits the pending component for at least pendingMinMs\nError → errorComponent on that route, or the root boundary\n404   → notFoundComponent, or a typed notFound() throw from a loader/guard\n```\n\n`pendingMs` (default debounce) prevents a fast route from flashing a skeleton; `pendingMinMs` floors the display so a very fast load does not flicker the skeleton away mid-paint. Errors thrown by loaders and guards land in the nearest `errorComponent`. Deferred segments that reject later settle into their own boundary with a retry. Uncaught errors bubble to the root boundary — the natural home for a root-level error screen. See [Streaming SSR](/docs/rendering/streaming) for deferred-error behavior.\n\n## beforeLoad Guards [#beforeload-guards]\n\n`beforeLoad` runs before loaders execute, which makes it the right place for authorization redirects. Guards run on the server during SSR and on the client during navigation — the same code path:\n\n```tsx title=\"beforeload-guards.tsx\"\nbeforeLoad: ({ session, location }) => {\n  if (!session.user) throw redirect({ to: '/login', search: { back: location.href } })\n}\n```\n\nAnything a guard throws stops the loaders for that route from running. Guards receive the full route context (`session`, `location`, `params`, `search`, `client`, `config`), so both auth redirects and permission checks are expressible in one place. See [Protecting Routes](/docs/auth/protecting-routes) for the broader authorization picture, and [Loaders & Data](/docs/frontend/loaders) for the guard-then-load ordering guarantee.\n\n## The Root Route [#the-root-route]\n\nEvery app has a root route at `src/ui/pages/__root.tsx`. It is an ordinary `definePage` file whose component establishes the provider tree and the outlet that nested routes render into:\n\n```tsx title=\"the-root-route.tsx\"\nimport { definePage, Providers, Outlet } from '@kwiva/react'\n\nexport default definePage({\n  component: () => (\n    <Providers theme=\"kwiva\">\n      <Layout>\n        <Outlet />\n      </Layout>\n    </Providers>\n  ),\n})\n```\n\n`Providers` injects session state, the typed client, the data-hook cache, internationalization, and theming into context — loaders and hooks consume them without manual wiring. The root route can define its own loaders and error boundary, which makes it the natural place for app-wide data. Anything mounted inside it (a sidebar, a topbar, a provider tree) wraps every route in the app. See [Nested Layouts](/docs/frontend/layouts) for layout composition.\n\n## Page Conventions [#page-conventions]\n\n* One `definePage` per file, named by directory — no manual registration\n* The file path determines the route; folder layout files compose around it\n* Loaders use the typed client, never raw `fetch` (enforced by the `no-raw-fetch-in-loaders` gates)\n* Components use data hooks over model identity — no hand-built query keys\n* No engine imports (the query engine is internal to the framework)\n* Types flow from the file to the loader, to the hooks, to the render — one type universe\n\n## What's Next [#whats-next]\n\n* [File-Based Routing](/docs/frontend/routing) — naming, dynamic segments, and route context\n* [Loaders & Data](/docs/frontend/loaders) — parallel and streamed data loading\n* [Navigation & Link](/docs/frontend/navigation) — typed links and programmatic navigation\n* [Nested Layouts](/docs/frontend/layouts) — composing pages with layout files\n* [Your First Page](/docs/getting-started/first-page) — build a page end to end\n";
